    "origin": "Kartik Purnima, celebrated on the full moon of the month of Kartika (Kartika Shukla Purnima), is an exceptionally revered day in Jainism, marking the conclusion of the four-month monsoon retreat (Chaturmas) and the reopening of the sacred pilgrimage routes to Shatrunjaya Hill in Palitana (Gujarat). According to Jain canonical history expounded in the Śatruñjaya Mahātmya and Acharya Hemachandra's commentaries, it was on this sacred tirthankara day that Dravida and Varikhila, the sons of King Bharata (the son of Adinatha), together with ten crore munis (ascetic monks), attained final liberation (Moksha) atop the peaks of Shatrunjaya. Furthermore, Bhagwan Adinatha, the first Tirthankara, made his first auspicious visit to the holy hill of Shatrunjaya on this full moon day. Because ascetics and lay pilgrims strictly refrain from climbing holy hills during the four monsoon months to avoid harming the millions of tiny insects and green vegetation flourishing in the rain, Kartik Purnima marks the joyful reopening of the pilgrim paths, where thousands of devotees assemble for the revered circumambulation (Chha Gau Pheri) spanning twelve miles around the hill, climbing barefoot in sacred silence. The sacred hill of Shatrunjaya boasts over eight hundred exquisite white marble temples, carved with intricate lace-like stone craftsmanship over centuries. As the monsoon retreat ends, thousands of pilgrims undertake the sacred circumambulation (Phari), chanting hymns of praise to the Tirthankaras and walking barefoot in reverence for the sanctified soil. The pilgrimage is undertaken with immense purity of intention, with devotees carrying minimal personal possessions and remaining absorbed in continuous internal contemplation (Japa).",
    "significance": "In Jainism, Shatrunjaya is regarded as the supreme tirtha (Siddhakshetra) where countless enlightened souls have cast off their worldly bodies to attain eternal liberation. The name itself means \"that which conquers internal enemies\" (Shatru = passions, Jaya = conquest). Visiting or spiritually contemplating Shatrunjaya on Kartik Purnima is believed to cleanse heavy karmas accumulated over lifetimes, because the hill vibrates with the ascetic energy of millions of liberated masters. Shatrunjaya is revered as the eternal mountain where the sound of the holy Namaskara Mantra resonates through every stone. The pilgrimage teaches that the arduous ascent of the steep mountain under the blazing sun is a mirror of the spiritual ascent of the soul: endurance, patience, and devotion transform physical struggle into spiritual liberation. It stands as a living testament to centuries of unwavering devotion, reminding each pilgrim that physical hardships endured in the pursuit of truth purify consciousness and reveal the soul's divine nature. For millions of householders who are physically unable to travel to Palitana, this day is celebrated as \"Dev Diwali\" by venerating sacred cloth paintings (Pata) depicting the temples and peaks of Shatrunjaya in their local derasar (temple). The festival teaches that external pilgrimage is a physical replica of the soul's internal ascent: climbing steep steps under the hot sun symbolizes enduring hardships with equanimity, while reaching the temple summits reflects the soul's arrival at liberation. The pilgrimage inspires devotees to cultivate unshakeable non-violence, mental serenity, and absolute detachment from worldly desires.",
