    "tradition": "buddhist",
    "origin": "Ullambana (Ghost Festival or Obon in Japan), celebrated on the fifteenth day of the seventh lunar month (August), is one of the most prominent festivals of filial piety in East Asian and Southeast Asian Buddhism. Rooted in the canonical Ullambana Sūtra and echoing the teachings of the Tirokuḍḍa Sutta of the Pali Canon, the festival traces back to Maudgalyayana (Moggallana), one of the Buddha's two chief disciples, who was renowned for his supreme mastery of psychic powers (Iddhi). Upon attaining arahantship, Maudgalyayana used his divine vision to locate his deceased mother, discovering to his horror that she had been reborn in the realm of hungry ghosts (Pretas) due to her past greed, with a neck thin as a needle and an enormous belly, unable to swallow food. When he attempted to feed her a bowl of rice using his psychic powers, the food burst into burning coals in her mouth. Weeping, he approached the Buddha, who explained that the karmic knot of past selfishness cannot be dissolved by one person's power alone. The Buddha explained to Maudgalyayana that when the Sangha completes its rains retreat on Pavarana, the accumulated spiritual purity and meditative attainments of hundreds of monastics create a vast reservoir of merit. By offering alms to this assembly with heartfelt devotion, the powerful spiritual energy radiates outward, reaching across realms to relieve ancestors from distress. Rather, on the day of Pavarana, when the Sangha completes its retreat, making collective offerings of food, robes, and medicine to the monks generates vast spiritual merit capable of releasing ancestors from lower realms. Following these instructions, Maudgalyayana's mother was miraculously delivered from her agonizing state.",
    "significance": "The Sanskrit word \"Ullambana\" (rendered in Chinese as Yulanpen) translates figuratively to \"delivering from being suspended upside down,\" depicting the excruciating distress of beings trapped in suffering states of consciousness. Ullambana elevates ancestor reverence into a profound practice of universal compassion and transfer of merit (Pattidana). The festival teaches that karmic energy is interconnected: by performing acts of genuine selflessness, feeding the hungry, and supporting the virtuous Sangha, practitioners can dedicate the resulting spiritual merit to relieve the suffering of departed loved ones. Ullambana deepens filial piety into universal compassion, teaching that our present life is inextricably linked to the sacrifices of countless generations who came before us. By offering merit to departed ancestors, practitioners heal generational trauma, dissolve residual grief, and awaken deep gratitude for the precious gift of human birth and spiritual guidance. It expands narrow familial love into boundless concern for all wandering beings who have died without family, protection, or peace. The festival combines deep filial gratitude with the contemplation of rebirth, reminding every seeker that all living beings have, at some point in the infinite cycles of samsara, been our own loving mothers and fathers. By performing charity and meditating on loving-kindness during Ullambana, practitioners dissolve ancestral grievances and nurture universal benevolence.",
