    "significance": "Guru Amar Das revolutionized Indian spiritual society by dismantling deep-rooted caste prejudices and gender discrimination. He established the sacred institution of Langar with the unyielding egalitarian rule: \"Pehle Pangat, Phache Sangat\" — every person, regardless of caste, creed, gender, or wealth, must first sit in the common row on the ground and eat simple vegetarian food together before being granted an audience with the Guru. When Mughal Emperor Akbar visited Goindval, he humbly complied, sitting on the floor with common peasants before meeting the Guru, deeply moved by the social equality of the sanctuary. The Guru fiercely condemned the brutal practices of Sati (burning widows alive) and Purdah (veiling women), advocating for female dignity and widow remarriage. He established twenty-two administrative dioceses (Manjis) and fifty-two sub-centers (Pirahs) across India to disseminate Gurbani, appointing qualified women as spiritual leaders and teachers. His sublime 40-stanza masterpiece, the Anand Sahib (Song of Bliss) composed in Raag Ramkali, reveals that true lasting joy is not an external sensory pursuit, but the quiet, unshakable awakening of the divine presence within the soul. Celebrating his Gurpurab inspires practitioners to live with egoless humility, serve society unconditionally, and champion universal equality across all communities.",
