    "origin": "Sri Guru Ram Das Ji, the fourth Sikh Guru, was born as Bhai Jetha in Chuna Mandi, Lahore, in 1534 and led the Sikh Panth from 1574 until his departure in 1581. Orphaned at the tender age of seven, the young Jetha earned a meager living by selling boiled chickpeas (ghungni) in village markets to support his younger siblings and grandmother. Deeply drawn to saintly fellowship, he arrived at Goindval Sahib, where he immersed himself in intense, selfless labor (seva), joyfully carrying heavy wicker baskets of mud on his head during the excavation of the sacred Baoli Sahib under Guru Amar Das. Seeing his boundless devotion, humility, and radiant spirituality, Guru Amar Das chose him as his son-in-law, marrying him to his saintly daughter Bibi Bhani, and later appointed him as his fourth spiritual successor, conferring upon him the name Ram Das (Servant of God). In 1577, Guru Ram Das acquired a tract of forested land surrounded by five sacred pools and founded the city of Ramdaspur (originally known as Chakk Ram Das or Guru Ka Chakk, which later flourished into Amritsar). With the voluntary labor of disciples from all walks of life, he excavated the central holy pool, the Amrit Sarovar (Pool of Nectar), transforming a quiet wilderness into the spiritual, economic, and cultural heart of the Sikh nation, inviting merchants, traders, and craftsmen of fifty-two different professions to settle in the new township. He laid down the foundational layout for the Harmandir Sahib, ensuring that the temple was built on a level lower than the surrounding city to symbolize that spiritual wisdom flows downward into humility, and designed with four entrances welcoming seekers from all four corners of the earth.",
    "significance": "Guru Ram Das embodied the sublime spiritual virtues of unconditional humility, divine sweetness, and ecstatic devotion. His profound poetry—comprising 679 sublime hymns across thirty classical raagas enshrined in the Sri Guru Granth Sahib—resonates with the intense longing of the soul to merge into the Divine Beloved. He composed the foundational four-stanza hymn of the Lavan in Raag Suhi, which forms the sacred core of the Sikh wedding ceremony (Anand Karaj), conceptualizing marriage not merely as a worldly social contract, but as a four-stage spiritual journey where two bodies merge into a single radiant soul anchored in the Divine. Guru Ram Das established the Masand system, appointing trustworthy community representatives to collect voluntary tithes (Dasvandh) and coordinate social welfare, laying the institutional foundation for the autonomous growth of the Panth. His life demonstrates that the deepest spiritual attainment arises not from high birth or intellectual arrogance, but from absolute humility, honest labor, and sincere love. Guru Ram Das personified the sacred truth that the divine light is realized through selfless surrender (Nimrata). His memory teaches that singing the Lord's praises in holy fellowship transforms the restless human heart into a tranquil sanctuary of divine peace. The sacred Sarovar he excavated continues to offer healing, peace, and spiritual renewal to millions of pilgrims, reminding humanity that singing the praises of the Lord in the company of the holy cleanses all inner impurities.",
